---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const app = appBySlug('privy')!;
const path = '/apps/privy';
const title = 'Privy for Shopify: current offering and legacy account continuity';
const desc = 'Evaluate capture-to-message handoffs while confirming which Privy account, listing and connection your store actually uses.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">App procurement directory</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Unranked operating guide · November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">
    <h2>Separate product scope from listing availability</h2>
    <p><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy’s current website</a> markets popups, email, SMS and journey automation. It therefore should not be described only as a capture accessory for another sender. Its published scope is a starting point for an account demonstration, not proof of every integration or destination.</p>
    <p>At this review, the <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/privy">legacy Privy Shopify listing</a> says the app is not currently available and directs support questions to Privy Operations. That listing state does not establish that Privy has closed or that existing accounts stopped sending. Ask Privy for the supported installation and connection path for your specific store.</p>
    <h2>Identify the account before changing it</h2>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Question for Privy</th><th>Evidence to retain</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Store connection</td><td>Which app identity and authorization belong to this store?</td><td>Store domain, account identifier and current connection status</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Product entitlement</td><td>Which capture, email and SMS functions are enabled under this agreement?</td><td>Plan terms and country eligibility</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Legacy continuity</td><td>Does the existing installation continue, require reconnection or move to another account?</td><td>Written instructions and named support owner</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Commercial continuity</td><td>What happens to billing and retained data after a connection change?</td><td>Invoices, renewal terms and export confirmation</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Do not uninstall a working legacy connection to try a new install route until its recovery path is understood. First inventory active forms, embeds, scripts, audiences and flows. Record which system sends each message; capture in one app and sending in another require an explicit permission handoff.</p>
    <h2>Trace capture through its first message</h2>
    <p>Use a test visitor who accepts email and declines SMS. Record the form version, source, disclosure and channel state. Confirm the email welcome reaches only the permitted destination and that a phone field does not silently become SMS subscription. Repeat with a returning customer so a new signup does not erase an existing preference or create a duplicate profile.</p>
    <p>Then exercise an intended SMS signup in an eligible market and inspect its destination account. If a second platform sends the welcome, verify identifiers, field mapping and suppression updates in both systems. An export count alone cannot prove the receiving audience has the right eligibility.</p>
    <h2>Resolve legacy forms and queued journeys</h2>
    <p>Map each popup and embedded form to its current destination, including mobile displays and old landing pages. Check whether disabling an old automation also cancels queued messages. Place a test order during a welcome or cart wait and inspect the next message before approving live traffic.</p>
    <p>If account continuity requires a rebuild, copy the business rule as well as the creative: entry source, waits, branches, purchase exits, discount eligibility and expiry. Keep the old and new sender from running the same job concurrently. Save an approved rollback action that does not reactivate obsolete messages.</p>
    <h2>Budget for the actual account boundary</h2>
    <p>Request current terms for contacts, email sends, SMS destinations and message units, onboarding and any overlapping account period. Confirm how duplicate and suppressed profiles affect billing. Reconcile the first invoice after a connection change against the agreed scope; a legacy listing price does not establish current terms.</p>
    <h2>Acceptance brief and sign-off</h2>
    <p>{app.shopifyFit}</p>
    <p>These are proposed checks, not observed merchant results. Approve only after the store connection, channel preferences, duplicate handling, purchase exits and billing owner have evidence. Keep open exceptions with a named owner and resolution date.</p>
    <h2>Common continuity questions</h2>
    <p><strong>Does an unavailable listing mean the product is closed?</strong> No. Compare the listing notice with the current vendor offering and obtain account-specific support guidance.</p>
    <p><strong>Should we add a second sender?</strong> First establish what your Privy account already owns. Add a sender only for a demonstrated requirement and define cross-system suppression before activating it.</p>
    <p>For related account questions, use the <a class="link" href="/apps/sendlane">Sendlane continuity review</a>. Plan the customer journey in the <a class="link" href="/use-cases">use-case hub</a> and use <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> when capture and sending have different owners.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
